\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{A common basis compares the two determinants}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{For an enriched object, choose one rational basis and use it for
both pairings. In that basis
##{\det(b_q)=\det(g_0)\det(Q).}
Each of these three scalars changes by a square under a change of
basis, so the square class of #{\det(b_q)} is the product of the
square classes of #{\det(g_0)} and #{\det Q}. The scalar
#{\det Q} alone does not supply #{\delta(q)} unless the
reference-pairing class is also known. This equality trivializes
the determinant line; it is not a canonical scalar determinant of
an operator from the space to its dual.}
